<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Grand Duchy of Luxembourg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxembourg, officially the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its recorded history begins in 963, when Siegfried, Count of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>, acquired a castle on the Bock rock, around which the County of Luxembourg grew within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. After French annexation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored it in 1815 in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 gave it modern borders by transferring its western part to Belgium. Independence and neutrality were reaffirmed after the Luxembourg Crisis, and in 1890 it gained its own monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxembourg.jpg" alt="Luxembourg" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Luxembourg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first traces of settlement date to the Palaeolithic about 35,000 years ago, and Celtic tribes settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> from the 6th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the Celtic tribes of this region the Treveri, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oppidum">Oppidum</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titelberg">Titelberg</a> is the best-known archaeological evidence of them. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 the territory belonged successively to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, Lotharingia and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p>When Henry VII became Emperor in 1312, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a> began to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John the Blind</a>, Count of Luxembourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, founded the Schueberfouer in 1340 and died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346; many historians consider him the epitome of chivalry, and he is a national hero. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a> inherited the Habsburg Netherlands, including the Duchy, in 1482. For nearly 320 years it was a Habsburg possession, under Austrian rule from 1506 to 1556, Spanish rule from 1556 to 1714 and Austrian rule again until 1794. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> invaded twice in 1542, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> reconquered it each time.</p><p>Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered most of the Duchy in 1793 and 1794, committing atrocities including the massacres of Differdange and Dudelange and the destruction of the abbeys of Clairefontaine, Echternach and Orval. The fortress held out for nearly seven months, leading Lazare Carnot to call it the best fortress in the world except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, which gave Luxembourg the nickname <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortress-of-luxembourg">Gibraltar of the North</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored the Duchy in 1815 as a Grand Duchy in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> under William I, ceding territory around Bitburg and St. Vith to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the second partition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded in August 1914, but Luxembourg kept much of its independence, and it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in April 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Luxembourg covers 2,586 square kilometres, at most 82 kilometres from north to south, and its capital is Luxembourg City, which hosts several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> institutions, including the Court of Justice. In 2025 foreign nationals made up 47 per cent of its 681,973 permanent residents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> is the sole national language, French is the language of legislation, and French, German and Luxembourgish are all used in administration. The constitution was originally adopted on 17 October 1868, and the old city and its fortifications became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luxembourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma’s musical life, shaped by its ducal theatres, helped nurture the operatic culture of the region, and the Bourbon-Parma family remains a well-known European royal house, related to the grand-ducal family of <b>Luxembourg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Several foreign monarchs were members through German possessions: the King of the United Kingdom as King of Hanover until 1837, the King of Denmark for Holstein and Lauenburg, and the King of the Netherlands for <b>Luxembourg</b> and, from 1839, Limburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schengen Agreement that abolished border checks across much of Europe is named after a small wine-growing village in <b>Luxembourg</b>, where it was signed on a boat on the Moselle in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>William I finally accepted the separation in the Treaty of London of 1839, which fixed the frontiers, divided Limburg and <b>Luxembourg</b>, and guaranteed Belgium’s perpetual neutrality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlement of 1839 left the northern kingdom with the provinces of the old Republic and the eastern half of Limburg, which was also a member of the German Confederation until 1866, while <b>Luxembourg</b> remained in personal union with the Dutch crown until 1890.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Slightly more than a quarter of the constitutional monarchies are in western Europe, among them the United Kingdom, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Norway, Sweden, Denmark, <b>Luxembourg</b>, Monaco and Liechtenstein, while the two most populous are in Asia: Japan and Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
